@* The noise record for one period — the verdicts, and every dispute raised against them.

   ★★ "PUBLISHES" MEANS A PERSON CAN READ IT. Disputes existed only as JSON at /api/noise/record/{period}, which
   makes the contest public to a vendor's engineer and to nobody else — and 01 §5 promises the contest is public,
   against published reasoning. This is the page that keeps that promise.

   ★★ AND THE UPHELD ONES ARE AS PROMINENT AS THE OVERTURNED. A contest that only appeared when the challenger
   won would be a complaints box; the upheld disputes are the evidence that this is not one.

   ★ Reads the store directly rather than fetching its own API: this IS CAI, so an HTTP call to itself would be a
   second path to the same rows and one more thing that can disagree. *@
@page "/noise/record"
@page "/noise/record/{Period}"
@using System.Globalization
@using Cai.Web.Noise
@inject INoiseStore Store

<PageTitle>CAI — noise record @Period</PageTitle>

<div class="hero"><div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">Noise record</p>
    <h1>@Period</h1>
    <p class="lede">
        Every verdict recorded for this period, and every dispute raised against them — upheld and overturned
        alike.
    </p>
</div></div>

@if (Period is not { Length: > 0 })
{
    @* ★★ AN INDEX, so the record is REACHABLE. A page that only answers /noise/record/{period} needs the reader
       to already know a period — and the layout deliberately carries no second navigation of its own, so
       without this the record is a URL you have to be told. *@
    var judged = Store.JudgedPeriods();

    <div class="hero"><div class="wrap">
        <p class="eyebrow">Noise record</p>
        <h1>The record, by period</h1>
        <p class="lede">
            Every period with judging recorded against it. Each one lists its verdicts and every dispute raised
            against them — upheld and overturned alike.
        </p>
    </div></div>

    <section data-region="record-index"><div class="wrap">
        @if (judged.Count == 0)
        {
            <p class="muted">
                <strong>No period has judging recorded yet.</strong> That is an absence, not a clean run.
            </p>
        }
        else
        {
            <ul>
                @foreach (var p in judged)
                {
                    <li><a href="@($"/noise/record/{p}")">@p</a></li>
                }
            </ul>
        }
    </div></section>

    return;
}

@{
    var resolutions = Store.ListResolutions(Period ?? "");
    var verdicts = Store.ListVerdicts(Period ?? "");
    var disputes = Store.ListDisputes(Period ?? "");
}

<section data-region="record-counts"><div class="wrap">
    <div class="counts">
        <span class="count">
            <span class="k">Findings judged</span>
            <span class="v">@resolutions.Count.ToString("N0", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)</span>
        </span>
        <span class="count">
            <span class="k">Raw verdicts</span>
            <span class="v">@verdicts.Count.ToString("N0", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)</span>
        </span>
        <span class="count">
            <span class="k">Disputes raised</span>
            <span class="v">@disputes.Count.ToString("N0", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)</span>
        </span>
        <span class="count">
            <span class="k">Still open</span>
            <span class="v">@disputes.Count(d => d.Outcome is null).ToString("N0", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)</span>
        </span>
    </div>

    @if (resolutions.Count == 0)
    {
        @* ★ An empty period says so. "Nothing has been judged here" and "everything was judged and agreed" are
           different facts, and a page of zeroes reads as the second. *@
        <p class="muted">
            <strong>No judging has been recorded for this period yet.</strong> That is an absence, not a clean
            run.
        </p>
    }
    else
    {
        <p class="muted">
            The verdict register is append-only. An overturned verdict is still in this record, with the dispute
            beside it — and an overturn does not change a published rate by itself: that takes a corrected
            publication, which the publication record shows as a correction.
        </p>
    }
</div></section>

<section data-region="disputes"><div class="wrap">
    <h2>Disputes</h2>

    @if (disputes.Count == 0)
    {
        <p class="muted">
            <strong>No verdict in this period has been contested.</strong> The route is
            <code>POST /api/noise/verdicts/{findingId}/dispute</code>; a dispute needs a reason, and its answer
            needs reasoning either way.
        </p>
    }
    else
    {
        <table class="lenses">
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Finding</th><th>Raised by</th><th>Reason</th><th>Outcome</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                @foreach (var d in disputes)
                {
                    <tr>
                        <td class="mono">@d.FindingId</td>
                        <td>@d.RaisedBy</td>
                        <td>@d.Reason</td>
                        <td>
                            @* ★★ THE STATE IS A LABEL, not an absence. An unanswered dispute is the one a reader
                               most needs to see: it is where the standard has been challenged and has not
                               replied. *@
                            <span class="pill @(d.Outcome ?? "open")">@Label(d.Outcome)</span>
                            <div class="muted">
                                @(d.ResolutionReasoning is { Length: > 0 } why ? why : "Not yet answered.")
                            </div>
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                }
            </tbody>
        </table>
    }

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>A dispute needs a reason, and an answer needs its reasoning — either way.</strong>
        An outcome with no reasoning is "the standard says so", which is not an argument the Code Assurance Index
        gets to make. The upheld disputes are published as prominently as the overturned ones; a contest that
        only appeared when the challenger won would be a complaints box.
    </div>
</div></section>

@code {
    /// <summary>The period, <c>yyyy-MM</c>.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Period { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The outcome as a word, "Open" while there is none.</summary>
    private static string Label(string? outcome) => outcome switch
    {
        "upheld" => "Upheld",
        "overturned" => "Overturned",
        _ => "Open",
    };
}
